(* ============================================================================
   8b. THE MARKDOWN TARGET'S AUTHORED ESCAPE NARROWS TOO -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-025]. §8a
      narrowed M1 and left M2 as written. M2 narrows on the same finding, and
      on a test of the same shape:

      M2a AN `escaped_text` NODE IS EMITTED AS AN ESCAPE WHEN ITS CHARACTER
          COULD BE READ AS MARKUP AT THE POSITION THE WRITER HAS REACHED ON
          THE EMITTED LINE, or when it is one of the smart-punctuation
          triggers `"`, `'`, `-` and `.`. Otherwise the character is emitted
          BARE.
      M2b `#` IS READ AS MARKUP ONLY AT A LINE'S CONTENT POSITION, where it
          opens an ATX heading. An `escaped_text` hash anywhere else on the
          line is emitted bare. A LINE'S CONTENT POSITION IS AFTER ITS
          CONTAINER PREFIX: the position is measured on the EMITTED LINE, so
          every prefix the writer put in front of the content is passed over
          before the test is decided -- a block quote marker, a list or task
          marker, a definition marker, the alignment §10 gives a continuation
          line -- in whatever combination and to whatever depth the writer
          emitted them. Column 0 is the content position of a line NO
          container encloses, and of no other line.
      M2c NOTHING ELSE NARROWS. M1, M3 and §8a are untouched, and a character
          Markdown reads as markup at that position keeps M2 as written.

        a -- b        ->  a – b
        a \-\- b      ->  a -- b

      `"`, `'`, `-` and `.` are not Markdown metacharacters, so M1 never
      reached them and M1's narrowing cannot either. M2a names them so the
      clause that narrows M2 cannot be read as dropping the reason M2 exists.

      THE `[` CASE §8a LEANS ON IS UNTOUCHED, and M2a is written positionally
      so that it is. A `[` can open a link at any position on a line, so M2a
      keeps its escape everywhere. `#` is the character that differs, because
      its reading is positional and every position but one is inert.

      A CONTAINER PREFIX IS PART OF THE LINE, WHICH IS WHY M2b MEASURES ON IT.
      M2b's position is the ONLY narrowing in this clause -- M2a keeps the
      escape by default and M2c closes the set. Read as an offset into the
      BLOCK'S CONTENT it answers a question no reader asks: the string a reader
      parses is the emitted line, and the two differ by exactly the prefix the
      writer put in front. §10 already decided this one character over, escaping
      a continuation line whose text reads as a list marker only because item
      alignment stands in front of it -- "§2 governs whether a character is
      escaped GIVEN the line the writer emits; §10 fixes the line."

      STATED OVER THE LINE, NOT AS A LIST OF CONTAINERS THAT COUNT:

      - A container this target FLATTENS contributes no prefix and needs no
        exemption. A colon fence emits no marker and a definition-list body
        emits no indent, so a hash in either lands at column 0 and its escape
        was already kept.
      - A container this target DOES prefix is covered whatever its kind,
        depth or mixture -- `> > `, `- > `, `- [ ] `, `[^a]: `, `   1. ` --
        without naming any of them.

      An enumeration would have to be revisited every time the writer gains a
      prefix. The line does not. Lazy continuation is not a case either: it is
      a PARSER concept, and this writer re-prefixes every line of the
      container, so M2b always sees the emitted prefix.

      NARROWING BY POSITION IS UNCHANGED, WHICH IS THE HALF THAT IS EASY TO
      LOSE. Moving the position does not widen M2b into "an escape behind a
      prefix is kept". The narrowing holds inside a container exactly as it
      does outside one, and so does a hash that stands AT the content position
      but opens no heading, since M2b's reading is CommonMark's and a run
      closed by a letter is not one:

        > C\# is a language      ->  > C# is a language
        - issue \#123 fixed      ->  - issue #123 fixed
        > \#tag rest             ->  > #tag rest

   9. THE MARKDOWN TARGET'S HARD BREAK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-026]. A `hard_break` is emitted
      as a BACKSLASH before the newline, never as two trailing spaces.

      Both spellings mean the same thing to a CommonMark reader. The difference
      is what survives handling: trailing whitespace is removed by editors that
      strip it on save, by `git apply --whitespace=fix`, and by CI whitespace
      checks -- and removing ONE of the two spaces is enough, so the break does
      not degrade, it VANISHES, silently, in a file nobody edited. The cost is
      pre-CommonMark processors, which show a literal backslash instead of a
      break: a visible artifact in a rare consumer, against silent data loss in
      a common one.

      This is a property of the MARKDOWN target only. The canonical writer spells
      a hard break the way the Carve grammar does (`hard_break`, PART 3), and
      §7's no-whitespace-only-line rule already covers the writer's side.

  10. A LIST ITEM'S CONTINUATION LINES ARE ALIGNED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-027]. Every
      continuation line of a list item is indented to the WIDTH OF THE MARKER,
      including one whose text would otherwise read as a list marker. Such a
      line is kept literal by ESCAPING it, per §2, not by indenting it less:

        1. outer
           1\. inner

      This does not conflict with §2. §2 governs whether a character is escaped
      GIVEN the line the writer emits; §10 fixes the line. A writer may not
      manufacture a layout whose only merit is dodging an escape.

  10a. AN UNUSED DEFINITION SURVIVES THE NON-HTML TARGETS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-028]. A
      FOOTNOTE or ABBREVIATION definition that NOTHING references is still
      emitted by the Markdown, plain-text and terminal renderers. HTML drops
      it, because HTML has nowhere to put a definition nobody used; the other
      three do not get to drop content the author wrote.

      A LINK REFERENCE DEFINITION IS COVERED TOO. It was excluded while it was
      the one definition kind with NO NODE IN THE TREE; PART 12 §10 gives it a
      node, so all three kinds walk and all three emit.

      THE GAP IS REAL AND IS NOT CLOSED BY NARROWING. An unused link reference
      definition survives neither the tree nor a round trip
      through the AST, and now explicitly not the non-HTML targets. So
      `carve --markdown` on a document that is only `[lbl]: /u` emits nothing,
      and the URL the author wrote is gone -- the same content loss §10a
      exists to prevent, reached by a different route.

          [^n]: %         ->  markdown  [^n]: %
                              plain     [^n]: %
                              html      (nothing)

      THE MARKER IS EMITTED AS WRITTEN. `[^n]: %` is a footnote definition, so
      it renders with its caret. `[n]: %` is not that construct -- it is a LINK
      reference definition, and emitting one where the author wrote the other
      turns a definition into something that parses back as a different
      construct.

  10b. A TABLE ROW KEEPS ITS OWN CELL COUNT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-029]. The canonical
      writer emits exactly the cells the row carries. It MAY align the padding
      of cells that exist, but MUST NOT append empty cells to make every row as
      wide as the widest row in the table. A missing trailing cell is not an
      empty cell: adding one changes both the re-parsed tree and the rendered
      table, so §1a makes rectangular alignment yield.

      Where a delimiter row is required to promote the first row to a header,
      that delimiter carries exactly one cell for each cell in the HEADER ROW,
      not one for each column reached by a wider body row. Its job is to mark
      that row as a header; extending it to the table width both manufactures
      header cells and can stop the delimiter from matching the row it belongs
      to. Native `=` header markers remain preferred wherever they can express
      the header, including when a later body row is wider.

  10c. ADJACENT BLOCKS THAT WOULD MERGE KEEP THE CONTINUATION MARKER --
       NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-030]. In a tight list item, deciding whether to retain `+` is a
       property of the emitted BLOCK SEQUENCE, not only of each child in
       isolation. If two adjacent children written at the item's content
       column would re-parse as one block, the writer emits each child from the
       first such child onward at the marker column, preceded by its own `+`.

       This includes adjacent block quotes, tables, line blocks, definition
       lists, and lists whose marker kind is compatible. A single block opener
       that cannot fold into the paragraph above it remains canonically
       indented at the content column. A blank line is not the fallback: it
       would make a tight item loose and change its rendered structure.


  10d. MULTIPLE CARVE HEADER ROWS SURVIVE THE MARKDOWN TARGET -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-031].
       Markdown pipe tables can identify only one header row. The first Carve
       header row therefore becomes that row, and every later Carve header row
       is emitted as an ordinary Markdown body row. Their header semantics are
       lost, but no authored cell content is dropped.

       The Markdown delimiter uses the final effective column alignment from
       the Carve header rows: a later explicit header marker wins, while an
       omitted marker does not reset an earlier one. A body-cell alignment is
       local to that cell and MUST NOT become the Markdown column alignment,
       including when that body row precedes the first header row.

  10e. A PRESENTATION TARGET KEEPS A CAPTION, A FENCE TITLE AND A FENCE
      LABEL -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-032]. `docs/graceful-degradation.md` states the floor for
      every non-interactive target: a renderer MUST preserve the construct's
      CONTENT and may drop only its INTERACTION, and authored text -- a label
      or a title that distinguishes one block from another -- MUST NOT be
      silently discarded.

      This clause decides only where the named text goes on the targets below.
      Unnamed construct/target pairs remain open, not allowed by omission.

      T1 A FENCE'S TITLE AND LABEL RENDER THE WAY A DIV'S ALREADY DO. This
         clause writes down an existing rule rather than a new one. A fenced
         div already surfaces both tokens on every target, which is the
         caption rule `docs/graceful-degradation.md` makes normative for an
         unconsumed label: `::: note "T" [L]` emits `**T**` then `**L**` in
         Markdown, a standalone line each in plain text, and a bold
         standalone line each on the terminal, the title always before the
         label. A code fence carries the SAME two tokens in the SAME two
         slots of its info string, so it renders them the same way, above the
         block:

             ```php "src/Auth.php" [Composer]   ->  src/Auth.php
             composer require x                     (blank)
             ```                                    Composer
                                                    (blank)
                                                    composer require x

      T2 A TABLE CAPTION SURVIVES THE MARKDOWN TARGET as body text AFTER the
         table, separated by one blank line. The position is what this target
         already does for the other two captioned hosts -- an image caption
         and a listing caption both follow their block as body text -- so the
         table was the odd one out rather than a consequence of Markdown
         having no caption syntax:

             |= H |             ->  | H |
             | a |                  | --- |
             ^ Table caption        | a |
                                    (blank)
                                    Table caption

      ADJACENCY IS NOT AVAILABLE HERE, AND THAT IS THE GENERAL FORM.
      A plain-text target has no delimiter to put around a block, so what it
      has instead is ADJACENCY: dropping the blank line says that the line
      below belongs to the block above. Attachment by adjacency is only
      available on a target where adjacency does not change what the adjacent
      block IS. A caption written directly after the last row of a Markdown
      table is read by a GFM reader as ANOTHER ROW, and a line written
      directly above a code block in plain text reads as the FIRST LINE OF THE
      CODE.

      TWO MORE LOSSES OF THE SAME TOKENS ARE RECORDED HERE AND LEFT OPEN. Each
      breaks the floor exactly as this clause's three tokens did; what is
      missing is a decided rendering, not a rule. Neither is settled by this
      clause and neither is thereby permitted:

        - A fence title on a fence with NO LANGUAGE is dropped by the
          MARKDOWN writer too: ```"src/app.js" emits a bare ``` and the
          title is gone. The Markdown target keeps the title only when a
          language precedes it.

        - A code-group label never reaches the HTML target at all:
          ```js [Node] gives `<pre><code class="language-js">`. Core
          assigns the label no meaning and a group extension consumes it, so
          on HTML with no such extension the token has nowhere to go. Where
          it should go is a question about the HTML target, which is PART
          10's business rather than this section's.

  10f. A REFERENCED ABBREVIATION DEFINITION SPLITS BY TARGET -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-033].
      §10a rules the definition NOTHING references: the Markdown, plain-text
      and terminal renderers all emit it, because those targets do not get to
      drop content the author wrote. It says nothing about the definition that
      IS referenced.

      This clause decides where the consumed definition line goes on those
      three targets and what plain text emits. Unnamed pairs remain open.

      T1 MARKDOWN KEEPS THE LINE, and keeps the expansion beside it.
         `*[TERM]: expansion` is the spelling PHP Markdown Extra uses
         for an abbreviation, so on this target the line is CONTENT rather
         than leaked source, and keeping it is what lets the export round-trip
         back to a working abbreviation. The duplication is the price of that
         round trip, and it is paid only here.

      T2 PLAIN TEXT AND THE TERMINAL DROP THE LINE and emit only the
         expansion, in the `TERM (expansion)` shape, at every occurrence:

             *[HTML]: Hyper Text  ->  plain     HTML (Hyper Text)
             (blank)                  terminal  HTML (Hyper Text)
             HTML

      THE TEST IS WHETHER THIS DEFINITION'S EXPANSION IS EMITTED, not whether
      its term appears. The line is dropped on plain and the terminal exactly
      where the same output carries the expansion that definition supplies,
      and kept otherwise. Three shapes fail the test, and in each the
      definition's text is carried by its own line and by nothing else, so
      dropping it would be the content loss §10a exists to prevent:

        - THE TERM NEVER APPEARS. That is §10a, which this clause leaves
          exactly as it stands.

        - AN AUTHORED `abbr` OUTRANKS THE DEFINITION. PART 9 §9 makes
          `[HTML]{abbr="Custom"}` authoritative and a resolved `abbreviation`
          child contributes only its visible text there, so the definition's
          own expansion reaches no target. `45-inline-extensions-11` pins
          `HTML (Custom)` on plain and on the terminal with the definition
          line above it, and this clause leaves that case unchanged.

        - A LATER DEFINITION OF THE SAME TERM WON. PART 9R R3 is last-wins, so
          in

              *[A]: a
              *[A]: b

              A here.

          only `b` is ever emitted. `*[A]: b` goes and `*[A]: a` stays, on
          plain and on the terminal alike. Dropping every definition of a term
          that expanded anywhere would delete the string `a` from the document
          outright, which is the loss this clause moves to prevent.

      THE MARKDOWN TARGET NEEDS NONE OF THAT TEST -- it keeps every definition
      line whatever became of it. So does the canonical writer, where PART 11
      §1's `parse(fmt(x)) == parse(x)` requires it, which leaves the round
      trip intact on both of the targets that have one.

  10g. A COMPOSITE FIGURE SURVIVES EVERY TARGET -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-034]
      (PART 9 §4c). The floor is `docs/graceful-degradation.md`'s: panel
      content, panel captions, preserved stray content and the group caption
      are all CONTENT, and no target may silently discard any of them. What
      this clause decides is the canonical writer's spelling and the three
      non-HTML renderings, so that engines degrade the construct identically
      instead of each meeting the floor its own way.

      THE CANONICAL WRITER EMITS THE AUTHORED FORM: the attribute line where
      attributes exist, the bare `::: figure` opener (`figure_group` has no
      title or label to spell), the children with one blank line between
      them, the closer at the opener's width (the inward-widening discipline
      of PART 9 §12 unchanged), and the group caption as a `^ ` line after
      the closer, its `#` placeholder written back as authored.

      THE CARET AFTER A GROUP CLOSER IS A CAPTION POSITION. §5's
      unconditional set escapes a leading caret in TEXT; the group caption is
      NOT text in that position -- it is the caption the closer hosts -- so
      the writer MUST NOT emit it as `\^ `, which would re-parse as a
      paragraph and break §1's `parse(fmt(x)) == parse(x)`. The other
      direction holds too: a PARAGRAPH whose text begins with a caret,
      sitting where §4's allowance reaches a group closer above it, keeps its
      `\^ ` escape, because bare it would re-parse as the group caption
      (corpus 318-composite-figures-6 holds the detached shape).

      T1 MARKDOWN emits the panels in order, each host degraded as it
         already does, each panel caption as an emphasized `*(...)*`
         paragraph after its host; preserved stray content in place; the
         group caption LAST, as a bold `**...**` paragraph, its number
         resolved. Emphasis and bold rather than invented syntax: Markdown
         has no figure grouping, and this is the spelling the tabs/label
         rule already uses for authored text with no native slot.

      T2 PLAIN TEXT AND THE TERMINAL put the group caption FIRST, its number
         resolved, then a blank line, then each panel: its caption line,
         then its host's degradation (`[Image: alt]` and the rest,
         unchanged), a blank line between panels. Caption-first because on a
         caption-less target the group caption is the only line that says
         what the following blocks are one of.

      Tabs, `presentation=` hints and PDF layout are renderer/extension
      business and out of this clause's scope; hint classes reach the HTML
      target only.

  10h. THE PLAIN-TEXT TARGET PRESERVES LIST DEPTH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-035]. A nested list
      item is indented by TWO ASCII SPACES for each list ancestor. The target
      may discard marker style and tight/loose layout, but it MUST NOT flatten
      parent, child, and grandchild items into siblings: indentation is the
      plain-text representation of that structure. ANSI and canonical Carve
      use the same two-space depth step; plain text differs only by omitting
      presentation styling, not by erasing hierarchy.

  10i. A HARD LIST BOUNDARY IS WRITTEN AS EXACTLY THREE BLANK LINES --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-036]. The writer normalizes a run of blank lines between two blocks
      to ONE. A run that is PART 9 §11 N1a's hard list boundary is the
      exception, and it is written as EXACTLY THREE -- not one, and not the run
      the author happened to type:

        - apples                 is written as:   - apples
        (six blank lines)                         (three blank lines)
        - oranges                                 - oranges

      ONE would MERGE the two lists, which §1 forbids: the boundary is the only
      thing keeping them apart, so the general normalization cannot reach this
      run. That much §1a already decides. THREE rather than the author's run is
      what this section adds, and it is a canonical-form choice: N1a reads
      THREE OR MORE, so every run at or above the threshold parses identically
      and §1 is indifferent between them. Left unstated, one document has
      unboundedly many canonical spellings, and `fmt` carries the author's
      whitespace into the one form whose purpose is not to.

      A RUN THAT IS NOT THE BOUNDARY NORMALIZES AS USUAL. Three blank lines
      between two paragraphs, or between two lists that already differ on an N1
      axis (`- a` then `* b`), separate nothing that one blank line does not
      separate as well, so they come back as one. What earns the three is the
      boundary's job, not the run's length.

  10j. AN UNSPELLABLE BLOCK DOES NOT CANCEL THE ADJACENCY IT CANNOT SPELL --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-037]. Where EVERY block between two sibling lists is
      UNSPELLABLE -- it reaches the writer from the AST and leaves no character
      on the page -- the two lists are ADJACENT for §10i's purposes, and
      §10i decides the run between them as if nothing had stood there.

      A whitespace-only paragraph has no Carve spelling, so it cannot separate
      the emitted lists. Without this rule they return with one blank line and
      merge into one loose list, violating §1.

      IT IS NOT A PARAGRAPH RULE. Any INTERCHANGE-ONLY block has this shape: a
      `figure` wrapping a `table` (PART 12 §17) is unspellable in the same
      sense and would cancel the same adjacency. So the rule is stated over what
      a block SPELLS and never over its type, and it reaches every such shape a
      later clause admits without naming one of them. A block that spells
      ANYTHING -- a thematic break, or a paragraph holding a NO-BREAK SPACE
      (§7) -- separates the two lists as it always did, and no boundary is
      written.

      NO CARVE SOURCE REACHES THIS, so nothing in the parse-driven corpus can
      pin it and the fixture is a hand-built payload entering through the AST
      ingest (tests/an-unspellable-block-does-not-cancel-list-adjacency.test.mjs).

  11. THE MARKDOWN TARGET'S CROSS-REFERENCES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-038]. A resolved
      cross-reference is emitted as a Markdown link to the target heading's id,
      and the target heading gains an explicit `{#id}` suffix. Both halves are
      required, and neither is emitted without the other:

        # H                       ->  # H {#H}
        See </#h>.                ->  See [H](#H).

      A heading NOTHING references gains no suffix. Markdown has no portable
      heading-id syntax, so an id every processor must be told about is noise
      unless something links to it.

      THE ID IS THE ONE THE DOCUMENT ASSIGNED, not a fresh slug of the heading
      text. The two differ whenever the core disambiguated a repeat (`Setup`
      and `Setup-2`), and a renderer that re-derives the slug does not merely
      mislabel the heading: it fails to recognize the reference as pointing at
      a known heading at all, so it drops the `{#id}` AND degrades the link to
      bare text -- one cause presenting as two unrelated symptoms.

      A CROSS-REFERENCE CONTRIBUTES NOTHING TO A HEADING'S SLUG. By the time
      this target runs, resolution has replaced `</#a>` with a link carrying
      the target heading's text, so counting it slugs `# A </#a>` as `A-A`.

      THE SCAN INCLUDES FOOTNOTE DEFINITION BODIES. They render as block
      content, so a reference inside one is a reference; missing them leaves a
      heading without the id that the link this target also emits depends on.

   ============================================================================ *)
